; touch_goodix_compile.pi4 - WHAT IS ON THE DISPLAY CONNECTOR'S I2C BUS, AND
; DOES THE TOUCH CONTROLLER ANSWER?
;
; DIAGNOSTIC. Does not ship.
;
; THE QUESTION. A Waveshare MIPI DSI panel puts two devices on the same
; I2C pair: a panel-control microcontroller at 0x45 and a Goodix touch
; controller at 0x14 or 0x5D. WHICH touch address answers, and what the
; product ID string reads, identifies the panel revision - and the panel
; revision decides whether the display needs a DSI initialisation table at
; all. So this probe answers a display question before it answers a touch
; one, and it is worth running even if nobody wants touch yet.
;
; It also exercises the repeated-start transfer I2cWriteRead(). Every
; register read below goes through it. The emitted-code model grades the
; bus shape; real-wire behavior remains a separate bench obligation.
;
; WHAT IT PRINTS
;   1. the bus, as found - which pins, on which function, at what rate
;   2. a scan of the four addresses that matter, and what each one means
;   3. the panel MCU: the opening sequence, a backlight ladder, enable
;   4. the touch controller: address, product ID, firmware version
;   5. the config block: the coordinate range, the contact slots and the
;      trigger type the controller reports about ITSELF
;   6. a bounded poll: every frame that arrives, in the controller's own
;      units
;
; Lines that assert start "  ok   " or "  FAIL "; the count is printed at
; the end. Nothing here loops forever.
;
; ***THERE IS NO SCREEN TRANSFORM IN THIS PROBE ANY MORE.*** Section 5
; used to check one, and both it and the driver's copy of it are gone:
; a contact becomes a screen pixel in the DISPLAY layer now, through the
; same rotation and scale the console's own pixels go through
; (RaspberryPi4/Board/screen_geom.pi4, ScrTouchX / ScrTouchY). It is
; graded at all four rotations and every corner by section J of
; pi4DsiScreenProbe.pi4, beside the pixel map it is the inverse of.
;
; WHICH BUS, AND WHY THIS ONE ON PURPOSE. i2c.pi4 drives BOTH BSC
; masters now - the monitor's own `touch` uses BSC0 on GPIO 44/45 at
; ALT1, the display connector's pair, with no jumper wires. THIS PROBE
; DELIBERATELY DOES NOT SELECT A BUS, so it runs on the library's
; default, the 40-pin header's BSC1 on GPIO 2 and 3. Two reasons:
;
;   a) it is the wiring the overlay's own i2c1 override describes - "Use
;      i2c-1 with jumper wires from GPIOs 2&3 (pins 3&5)" - which is the
;      cheapest way to put a touch controller on a wire with no DSI link
;      at all;
;   b) it is the bus tools/a64/touch_emitted_check.py models, register by
;      register. A probe that selected the connector's bus would be
;      ungradeable on a machine with no BCM2711 in it.
;
;   SO ON A STOCK BOARD WITH THE PANEL IN THE DISPLAY SOCKET AND NO
;   JUMPERS, THIS PROBE FINDS NOTHING, AND THAT IS NOT A FAULT.
;
; It prints the pins it is actually using, so a run that found nothing
; cannot be mistaken for a run against the right wires.
;
; ***IT DRIVES THE v1 PANEL MICRO, AND THE BENCH PANEL IS A v2.*** That
; is deliberate and it is a limit worth knowing before running it: the
; v1 register file at $AB/$AD/$C0/$C2/$AC is OUTSIDE the v2 micro's
; regmap, and on a v2 board the Goodix is held in reset by that micro's
; expander bit 9 until it is written the v2 way - so this probe will not
; wake the touch controller on the panel that is plugged in. This file
; is the vehicle the emulator gate drives against a modelled bus, and
; the instrument for the real panel is the monitor's own `touch`.
;
; BUILT WITH --entry-returns, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS SAFE TO RUN. It is sent
; to a resident Anvil and RETURNS to it, so a wrong answer costs a few
; lines of output rather than a power cycle:
;
;   PureMetalForge.exe --compile RaspberryPi4/Tests/touch_goodix_compile.pi4 -t pi4 \
;        --load-addr 0x400000 --stack-addr 0x3000000 --entry-returns \
;        -o touch.img
;
; ARM THE DEADMAN FIRST. `deadman 15` immediately before `run 400000`.
;
; WHAT IT LEAVES BEHIND. The I2C pins on alt0 and the divider as it was
; found. If a panel MCU answered, THE BACKLIGHT IS LEFT ON AND THE PANEL
; LEFT ENABLED - deliberately, because a probe that lights a screen and
; then darkens it before anyone looks has proved nothing to the one
; instrument that can judge it.

; THE ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING (house rule 10 - a library never includes a
; library, so this file lists every one it needs, in a sequence that
; works). i2c.pi4 calls PinAlt out of gpio.pi4 and the two clock getters
; out of mailbox.pi4, so both must be parsed BEFORE it. dsi_panel.pi4 and
; touch_goodix.pi4 both call into i2c.pi4, so they come after it.
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/timer.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/gpio.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/i2c.pi4"
; The model also grades the legacy v1 MCU sequence. It is a TEST FIXTURE,
; not the v2 backend the shipping board uses.
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Tests/Fixtures/dsi_panel_v1.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/touch_goodix.pi4"

#UART_DR = $FE201000
#UART_FR = $FE201018

; How many times to ask for a frame before giving up. The buffer-ready bit
; lags a touch by about ten milliseconds and there may be no finger at
; all, so this is a bound and not an expectation: running out is a normal
; result and is printed as one.
#TP_POLLS = 240

; Microseconds between polls. Linux polls this controller at 17 ms for
; "60fps"; 20 ms is 50 Hz, which is faster than a finger and slower than
; anything that would starve the bus.
#TP_POLL_US = 20000

Global tpFails.i
Global tpChecks.i

; ======================================================================
;  Output. The same PL011 primitives every diagnostic in this directory
;  carries; they are repeated rather than shared because a diagnostic that
;  needs a library to print cannot report a failure in that library.
; ======================================================================

Procedure PutC(c.i)
  Define f.i
  Repeat
    f = PeekN(#UART_FR)
  Until (f & 32) = 0
  PokeL(#UART_DR, c)
EndProcedure

Procedure PutS(*s)
  Define i.i
  Define c.i
  i = 0
  Repeat
    c = PeekA(*s + i)
    If c = 0 : Break : EndIf
    PutC(c)
    i = i + 1
  ForEver
EndProcedure

Procedure PutNl()
  PutC(13) : PutC(10)
EndProcedure

Procedure PutDec(v.i)
  Define d.i
  Define started.i
  Define digit.i
  If v < 0
    PutC(45)
    v = 0 - v
  EndIf
  If v = 0
    PutC(48)
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  d = 1000000000000000000
  started = 0
  While d > 0
    digit = (v / d) % 10
    If digit <> 0 Or started = 1
      PutC(48 + digit)
      started = 1
    EndIf
    d = d / 10
  Wend
EndProcedure

Procedure PutHex(v.i, digits.i)
  Define i.i
  Define nib.i
  i = digits - 1
  While i >= 0
    nib = (v >> (i * 4)) & $F
    If nib < 10
      PutC(48 + nib)
    Else
      PutC(55 + nib)
    EndIf
    i = i - 1
  Wend
EndProcedure

Procedure Row(*name, v.i)
  PutS("  ")
  PutS(*name)
  PutS(" = ")
  PutDec(v)
  PutS("   ($")
  PutHex(v, 8)
  PutS(")")
  PutNl()
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  ASSERTIONS. Every one prints its own line whether it passes or fails,
;  because a diagnostic that only speaks up when something is wrong cannot
;  be distinguished from one that did not run.
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i Chk(*name, got.i, want.i)
  tpChecks = tpChecks + 1
  If got = want
    PutS("  ok   ")
    PutS(*name)
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  tpFails = tpFails + 1
  PutS("  FAIL ")
  PutS(*name)
  PutS("   got ")
  PutDec(got)
  PutS("  want ")
  PutDec(want)
  PutNl()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; A NOTE is a finding, not a verdict. Nothing that depends on a panel
; being plugged in is allowed to be a Chk, or the probe would report FAIL
; for the ordinary case of a bench with no panel on it.
Procedure Note(*s)
  PutS("  note ")
  PutS(*s)
  PutNl()
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  What each address on this bus would mean.
; ======================================================================

Procedure SayAddress(a.i)
  PutS("  $")
  PutHex(a, 2)
  PutS("  ")
  If a = $45
    PutS("a Waveshare panel-control MCU (v1) or GPIO expander (v2)")
  ElseIf a = $14
    PutS("a Goodix GT911 - the v1 panel, 1280x800 landscape")
  ElseIf a = $5D
    PutS("a Goodix GT9271 - the v2 panel, 800x1280 portrait")
  ElseIf a = $41
    PutS("an Ilitek ILI251x - a 13.3 inch board")
  Else
    PutS("something this probe does not have a name for")
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE CONFIG BLOCK - the numbers every mapped coordinate is scaled by.
;
;  ***THE SCREEN TRANSFORM IS NOT IN THIS PROBE ANY MORE, AND IT IS NOT
;  IN THE DRIVER EITHER.*** It moved into the display layer, where the
;  console's own pixels are turned - RaspberryPi4/Board/screen_geom.pi4,
;  ScrTouchX / ScrTouchY - because there is ONE rotation on this board
;  and a touch path with a second one would disagree with the picture
;  the first time somebody typed `screen rotate`. It is graded, at all
;  four rotations and at every corner, by section J of
;  RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/pi4DsiScreenProbe.pi4 under
;  tools/a64/a64_dsiscreen_check.py.
;
;  What IS this probe's business is the numbers the controller itself
;  reports, because they are the input to that mapping and a wrong one
;  does not look like an error - it looks like a finger landing a hand's
;  width from where it touched.
;
;  goodix.c:1072 reads exactly these, at exactly these offsets:
;      [0]     the config VERSION
;      [1..2]  x_max, little-endian        RESOLUTION_LOC
;      [3..4]  y_max, little-endian
;      [5]     max contacts, low nibble    MAX_CONTACTS_LOC
;      [6]     trigger type, low two bits  TRIGGER_LOC
; ======================================================================

Procedure CheckConfig()
  PutS("-- 5. the config block the controller reports --")
  PutNl()

  If Gt9Addr() = 0
    Note("skipped - no controller answered.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  Row("config version", Gt9ConfigVersion())
  Row("x range", Gt9XMax())
  Row("y range", Gt9YMax())
  Row("contact slots", Gt9MaxPoints())
  Row("trigger type asked for", Gt9IntTrigger())

  ; A RANGE OF ZERO IS NOT A COORDINATE SPACE. goodix.c:1213 treats
  ; exactly this as "invalid config" and falls back to a default; this
  ; driver does not invent a number, because an invented range puts
  ; every finger in the wrong place with nothing reporting an error.
  Chk("the x range is a usable number", Gt9XMax() > 0, 1)
  Chk("the y range is a usable number", Gt9YMax() > 0, 1)

  ; goodix.c:1093 - min(config[5] & 0x0f, GOODIX_MAX_CONTACTS).
  Chk("the contact count is at least one", Gt9MaxPoints() >= 1, 1)
  Chk("and no more than the part's ten", Gt9MaxPoints() <= 10, 1)

  ; goodix.c:1092 - config[6] & 0x03, so it cannot exceed 3.
  Chk("the trigger type is in range", Gt9IntTrigger() <= 3, 1)
  Note("nothing on this board is wired to that trigger, so the driver")
  Note("polls - which is what the reference driver does on this wiring.")
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================

Procedure.i Main()
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  Define a.i
  Define found.i
  Define hz.i
  Define addr.i
  Define polls.i
  Define frames.i
  Define r.i
  Define *idp

  tpFails = 0
  tpChecks = 0

  PutNl()
  PutS("=== touch_goodix_compile - the display connector's I2C bus ===")
  PutNl()
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  PutS("-- 1. the bus, as found --")
  PutNl()

  I2cUp()
  Row("sda pin", #I2C_PIN_SDA)
  Row("scl pin", #I2C_PIN_SCL)
  Chk("both pads are on their alt function", I2cPinsMuxed(), 1)
  hz = I2cGetSpeed()
  Row("scl hz", hz)
  Row("divider source hz", I2cSourceHz())
  Row("timeouts since boot", I2cTimeoutCount())

  Note("these are GPIO 2 and 3 - the 40-pin header, not the display FPC.")
  Note("the panel reaches BSC0 on GPIO 44/45 at ALT1, and i2c.pi4 drives")
  Note("that master too - but this probe stays on the header bus on")
  Note("purpose, because that is the wiring the overlay's own i2c1")
  Note("override describes and the one the emulator gate models. Jumper")
  Note("the panel to header pins 3 and 5 to use this probe, or use the")
  Note("monitor's own `touch`, which drives the connector's bus.")
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  PutS("-- 2. who is on the bus --")
  PutNl()

  found = 0
  i = 0
  While i < 4
    If i = 0
      a = $14
    ElseIf i = 1
      a = $41
    ElseIf i = 2
      a = $45
    Else
      a = $5D
    EndIf
    r = I2cProbe(a)
    SayAddress(a)
    If r = #I2C_OK
      PutS("   ANSWERED")
      found = found + 1
    ElseIf r = #I2C_NACK
      PutS("   -")
    Else
      PutS("   bus fault, code ")
      PutDec(r)
    EndIf
    PutNl()
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  Row("addresses that answered", found)
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  PutS("-- 3. the panel control MCU at $45 --")
  PutNl()

  If DsiPanelBegin() = 0
    Note("no MCU answered the opening sequence. Nothing was enabled.")
    Row("last bus code", DsiPanelBusCode())
  Else
    PutS("  ok   the opening sequence was acknowledged")
    PutNl()
    Chk("three writes went out", DsiPanelWriteCount(), 3)

    ; A ladder rather than one value, because a backlight that only ever
    ; sees 255 cannot show whether the inversion is right way round: 0 and
    ; 255 are each other's mirror image and both "work".
    If DsiPanelBacklight(0) <> 0
      Note("backlight asked for 0 - the panel should be DARK now")
      delay(400)
    EndIf
    If DsiPanelBacklight(128) <> 0
      Note("backlight asked for 128 - the panel should be HALF now")
      delay(400)
    EndIf
    If DsiPanelBacklight(255) <> 0
      Note("backlight asked for 255 - the panel should be FULL now")
    EndIf
    Chk("the level the driver last asked for", DsiPanelBacklightAsked(), 255)
    ; Three opening writes plus two per backlight step, three steps.
    Chk("nine writes have gone out", DsiPanelWriteCount(), 9)

    If DsiPanelEnable(1) <> 0
      PutS("  ok   the panel was enabled")
      PutNl()
    EndIf
    Chk("the enable value last written", DsiPanelEnableAsked(), 1)
  EndIf
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  PutS("-- 4. the touch controller --")
  PutNl()

  addr = Gt9Begin()
  If addr = 0
    Note("no Goodix answered at $5D or $14.")
    Row("code at $5D", Gt9CodeHigh())
    Row("code at $14", Gt9CodeLow())
    Note("a code of -2 is a NACK, which means nothing is there;")
    Note("-8 means the repeated start could not be issued, which would")
    Note("be a defect in I2cWriteRead and not a missing panel.")
  Else
    Row("address", addr)
    PutS("  product id = ")
    *idp = Gt9ProdId()
    If *idp <> 0
      PutS(*idp)
    EndIf
    PutNl()
    Row("firmware version", Gt9Firmware())
    Row("config version", Gt9ConfigVersion())
    If addr = $14
      Note("$14 and a GT911 means the v1 panel: 1280x800, and the display")
      Note("needs NO DSI initialisation sequence.")
    Else
      Note("$5D and a GT9271 means the v2 panel: 800x1280, and the display")
      Note("needs a long DCS init table this tree does not have.")
    EndIf
  EndIf
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  CheckConfig()
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  PutS("-- 6. frames --")
  PutNl()

  If addr = 0
    Note("skipped - no controller answered.")
  Else
    polls = 0
    frames = 0
    While polls < #TP_POLLS
      n = Gt9Poll()
      If n >= 0
        frames = frames + 1
        If n > 0
          PutS("  frame with ")
          PutDec(n)
          PutS(" touch(es)")
          PutNl()
          i = 0
          While i < n
            PutS("    id ")
            PutDec(Gt9Id(i))
            PutS("  raw ")
            PutDec(Gt9RawX(i))
            PutS(",")
            PutDec(Gt9RawY(i))
            PutS("  size ")
            PutDec(Gt9Size(i))
            PutNl()
            i = i + 1
          Wend
        EndIf
      ElseIf n = #GT9_EPROTO
        Note("a frame claimed more than ten touches and was refused.")
      ElseIf n = #GT9_EBUS
        Note("a transfer failed mid-frame.")
        Row("last bus code", Gt9LastBusCode())
      EndIf
      delay(#TP_POLL_US / 1000)
      polls = polls + 1
    Wend
    Row("polls", polls)
    Row("frames seen", Gt9Frames())
    Row("frames refused for an impossible count", Gt9BadFrames())
    If Gt9Frames() = 0
      Note("no frame ever became ready. With a controller that answered")
      Note("its product id, that means nothing touched the panel - or the")
      Note("buffer-ready bit is never being cleared, which would show as")
      Note("the FIRST frame arriving and then nothing.")
    EndIf
  EndIf
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Row("checks", tpChecks)
  Row("failures", tpFails)
  If tpFails = 0
    PutS("=== done, no failures ===")
  Else
    PutS("=== done, WITH FAILURES ===")
  EndIf
  PutNl()
  ProcedureReturn tpFails
EndProcedure
